@page "/page-audits"
<PageTitle>Page audits · Blazor Accessibility Toolkit</PageTitle>

<div class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Browser tests · Playwright</p>
    <h1>Page audits</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        <code>page.AuditAccessibilityAsync()</code> audits a page of your running app in a real browser: axe-core 4.14
        against WCAG 2.2 A and AA, plus the toolkit's browser diagnostics, such as an <code>aria-expanded</code> that
        disagrees with what is visible. The examples below open pages of this very app.
    </p>
</div>

<RunAllTests />

<CodeBlock Caption="Page audits in an end-to-end test" Code="@Shape" />

<section aria-labelledby="whole-page">
    <h2 id="whole-page">Audit a whole page</h2>
    <p>
        The <a href="legacy">legacy page</a> stands for a page of an existing app. It uses two old components: an icon
        button without a name, and a card that opens on click. The page audit finds the button. It does not find the
        card: axe-core cannot see Blazor's <code>@@onclick</code> handler in the DOM, which is why the
        <a href="blazor-rules">Blazor-aware rules</a> audit the render tree with bUnit.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A page with a known issue" Defect="true" Example="PageAuditExamples.LegacyPageAudit"
                 Hears="button" HearsLabel="The icon button is announced as" Affects="@(["Screen reader users", "Voice control users"])">
        <Stage>
            <div class="legacy-widgets">
                <UnlabelledIconButton />
                <ClickableCard Title="Old card" Details="Only mouse users can open this card." />
            </div>
        </Stage>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="suppressions">
    <h2 id="suppressions">Accept a known issue, with a reason and a deadline</h2>
    <p>
        Sometimes a known issue cannot be fixed right away. A <code>Suppression</code> accepts it in the test code: it
        needs a reason, can name an owner and has a last day. Suppressed findings stay in the audit and in every report;
        they just do not fail the test. A suppression should be as narrow as possible: this one matches one rule on one
        selector. For accessibility debt across a whole app, a <a href="ci-gate">baseline</a> is usually the better tool.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A documented suppression" Tag="Known issue, suppressed" Example="PageAuditExamples.LegacyPageSuppression">
        <ChildContent><p>The test passes, and the audit still lists the button, marked as suppressed with its reason.</p></ChildContent>
    </ExampleCard>
    <ExampleCard Title="An expired suppression" Defect="true" Tag="Expired suppression" Example="PageAuditExamples.ExpiredSuppression">
        <ChildContent><p>The same suppression, one day after its last day. It stops applying, so a deadline cannot be forgotten.</p></ChildContent>
    </ExampleCard>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="scope">
    <h2 id="scope">Choose what to audit</h2>
    <p>
        Options select the rules (<code>Tags</code>, <code>EnabledRules</code>, <code>DisabledRules</code>,
        <code>IncludeBestPractices</code>) and the part of the page (<code>ExcludeSelectors</code>, or audit a single
        region with <code>locator.AuditAccessibilityAsync()</code>). The end-to-end tests of this showcase audit every
        page this way, in both themes: the intentional defects are excluded, everything else must pass.
    </p>
    <ExampleCard Title="A showcase page with its defects excluded" Example="PageAuditExamples.ShowcasePageAudit" />
</section>

@code {
    private const string Shape = """
        // A fixture starts the app (WebApplicationFactory on Kestrel) and a browser; see the demo's end-to-end tests.
        await using var session = await app.OpenAsync("/checkout");

        var audit = await session.Page.AuditAccessibilityAsync(
            new AccessibilityAuditOptions { ExcludeSelectors = ["#third-party-widget"] },
            new AuditLabel { Scenario = "checkout", State = "loaded" });   // the label appears in reports and baselines

        audit.ShouldHaveNoViolations();
        """;
}
